<!--
  The launcher mark: the brand's wheat ear, in white, over the red the adaptive icon paints beneath it.

  The ear is the "I" of the logomarca, and it is the one element of that logo that survives being small.
  The wordmark is far too wide for a launcher icon and the tricolor ribbon is three hairlines at 48 dp,
  so the icon is the ear alone, on the ground the ear was designed on.

  Four choices, each made for launcher size rather than for logo size:

  - The ear stands vertical. The logo's ear leans, and a 7 degree lean was drawn and rejected: at 48 dp
    it reads as a misprint rather than as a gesture, and every mask the system applies, whether circle,
    squircle or rounded square, is symmetric about the vertical axis, so a leaning mark sits off-centre
    under all three. Vertical spends the safe zone's height on the mark instead of on the lean.

  - Each grain's awn is the continuation of that grain's own taper, not a separate hairline. The logo
    draws long, elegant awns; at 48 dp a hairline is gone, leaving disconnected specks. Fused, the awn
    is attached mass and still tapers to a point.

  - The grain is plump but pinches to a narrow neck where it meets the stem. Where consecutive spikelets
    sit on their full width they fuse into one jagged blade and the ear reads as a leaf or a flame; the
    neck is what leaves a deep notch between them, so the pairing, the thing that says "wheat", is still
    legible once the whole mark is 29 px tall.

  - The stem is left bare below the lowest pair and ends in a blunt cut foot. The bare stem is the other
    half of what separates an ear of wheat from a leaf, and the blunt foot replaces a needle-thin base
    that is sub-pixel at 48 dp and left the ear looking unrooted.

  Geometry, in this 108x108 viewport: the mark spans x 40.99..67.01 and y 20.50..87.03, and every point
  of it lies within 33.5 units of the centre (54,54). The adaptive icon's guaranteed-visible area is the
  middle 72x72, i.e. 18..90 on both axes with half-side 36, and the 72-diameter guaranteed circle has
  radius 36, so the mark clears both by 2.5 units and no mask can reach it. It is deliberately not sized
  to the full viewport: a shape that fills 108x108 has its tips cropped on every launcher.

  Each spikelet is its own <path>. They are the same opaque colour, so the overlaps are invisible, and
  separate paths mean two shapes can never interact through the fill rule and punch a hole in the mark.
-->
